course: books
lesson: 52
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter VIII, part 1: Toad's Adventures (the gaoler's daughter and the buttered toast)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Toad, locked in the dark castle dungeon, throws himself on the floor and weeps for weeks; now he praises «wise old Badger» and the «clever, intelligent Rat and sensible Mole», calls himself a «stupid animal», and will not eat.
explain: The gaoler's kind daughter, who loves animals, asks to look after him. She brings him bubble-and-squeak, and the smell of hot cabbage makes him dream of meadows, gardens and the cheerful dinners at Toad Hall.
explain: Later she brings tea and thick, hot buttered toast; Toad sits up, eats, and soon starts boasting about himself, his house and how important he is.
explain: When he describes Toad Hall like an advert for selling a house, she laughs: «I don't want to take it. Tell me something real about it.» So he tells her about the boathouse, the gardens, the banqueting-hall and his animal friends.
explain: By bedtime he is his old cheerful, self-satisfied self, sings a little song and sleeps well; as the days pass the girl feels sorry for him, and vain Toad thinks she admires him enormously.
fact: «Immured in a dank and noisome dungeon»: immured = walled up; dank = cold and damp; noisome = smelly and nasty.
fact: Toad wails: «This is the end of everything ... at least it is the end of the career of Toad, which is the same thing»: to him, his own troubles are the end of the world.
fact: Now he praises his friends: «O wise old Badger! ... O clever, intelligent Rat and sensible Mole!» In chapter VI he had called the Rat a fellow with «very little intelligence and absolutely no education».
fact: For several weeks he refuses «his meals or intermediate light refreshments» (snacks between meals).
fact: The gaoler (an old spelling of jailer, said the same way) hints that comforts could be sent in «at a price», because Toad's pockets are «well lined» (full of money).
fact: The gaoler's daughter is «a pleasant wench and good-hearted»; «wench» is an old word for a girl or young woman, which today can sound rude.
fact: She keeps a canary, «several piebald mice» (patchy black and white) and «a restless revolving squirrel» (in a turning cage); at night the canary's cage is covered with an antimacassar (a cloth for the back of a chair).
fact: She says to her father: «Father! I can't bear to see that poor beast so unhappy ... You let me have the managing of him.»
fact: She says: «I'll make him eat from my hand, and sit up, and do all sorts of things»: she thinks of him rather like a pet, but wisely never tells him so.
fact: Her first dish is bubble-and-squeak (fried leftover cabbage and potato); the smell of hot cabbage gives Toad «new and inspiring thoughts».
fact: Next comes tea and buttered toast, «the butter running through the holes in it in great golden drops, like honey from the honeycomb»; «The smell of that buttered toast simply talked to Toad».
fact: Toad describes his house like an estate agent's advert: «an eligible self-contained gentleman's residence very unique ... Up-to-date sanitation. Five minutes from church, post-office, and golf-links». «Very unique» is a funny mistake, because unique already means the only one of its kind.
fact: She laughs: «Bless the animal ... I don't want to take it. Tell me something real about it.»
fact: She lets him talk on and on, because she sees that talking about himself does him as much good as the tea.
fact: He tells her about the boathouse, the fish-pond, the walled kitchen-garden, the pig-styes, the stables, the dairy, the linen-presses («she liked that bit especially») and the banqueting-hall.
fact: By bedtime he is again «the same sanguine, self-satisfied animal that he had been of old» (sanguine = cheerful and hopeful).
fact: Toad thinks «the social gulf between them was so very wide», because he is rich and she is a gaoler's daughter: an old, snobbish idea that some people are «above» others, which is wrong.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «mardy» (Midlands) = sulky; «peng» (London) = tasty, great; «ate» = did brilliantly; «dreich» (Scotland) = dreary; «don't have a cow» (older US) = calm down.
exercise: Where is Toad at the start of chapter VIII? | In a dungeon in a grim old castle | In his own bedroom at Toad Hall, locked in by Badger | In a little cell at the village police-station | In Badger's study, with a red handkerchief over his face
exercise: What does Toad do at first in the dungeon? | Lies on the floor and weeps in despair | Starts digging a tunnel out with a spoon from his dinner | Sings and dances to annoy the gaoler | Writes long, angry letters to his lawyer every day
exercise: Which friends does Toad now call wise, clever and sensible? | Badger, the Rat and the Mole | Otter, little Portly and the field-mice | The magistrates and the Clerk of the court | The chauffeur and the fat red-faced policemen
exercise: How long does Toad weep and refuse his meals? | Several weeks | One afternoon | Twenty whole years | Just two days and a night
exercise: What does the gaoler point out to Toad? | Comforts can be sent in from outside, at a price | Toad will be set free next Monday morning if he is good | Toad must scrub the castle kitchens every single day | Toad's three friends are coming soon to visit him
exercise: «Toad's pockets were well lined.» What does that mean? | He had plenty of money | His coat had warm silk sewn inside it | He had stuffed his pockets full of bread | His pockets were full of holes
exercise: Who asks to have «the managing of him»? | The gaoler's daughter | The gaoler's old wife | A kind young policeman | The washerwoman aunt
exercise: Which pets does the girl keep? | A canary, piebald mice and a squirrel | A dog, a parrot and a bowl of goldfish | A kitten, a white rabbit and a tortoise | Three hedgehogs and a very small mole
exercise: Why does she want to look after Toad? | She loves animals and pities him getting so thin | She wants his money and his big house for herself | Her father ordered her to keep a close watch on him | She wants him to do the castle's washing for her
exercise: What does she bring him first? | Bubble-and-squeak | Bread and water on a tin plate | A bowl of oatmeal porridge | Sardines and captain's biscuits
exercise: What is bubble-and-squeak? | Fried cabbage and potato | A sweet fizzy drink | A squeaky rubber toy | Boiled fish in a bubbly sauce
exercise: What does the smell of hot cabbage make Toad think of? | Meadows, gardens, dinners at home, his cleverness | The motor-car, the open road and the poop-poop | The Wild Wood at night and the weasels' faces | The court-room, the Chairman and the twenty years
exercise: What does she bring the second time? | Tea and hot buttered toast | Cocoa and a slice of plum-cake | Milk and a cheese sandwich | Soup and a crust of bread
exercise: «The smell of that buttered toast simply talked to Toad.» What did it talk of? | Warm kitchens and cosy firesides | Iron bars, heavy chains and bread and water | The blue sea and far-off sunny countries | Motor-cars, speed and the dusty high road
exercise: What does Toad do once he has eaten? | Talks about himself, his house and how important he is | Falls fast asleep without saying a single word | Asks the girl lots of questions about her own family and her pets | Cries harder than ever, thinking of home
exercise: How does Toad first describe Toad Hall? | Like an advert for selling a house | Like a fairy-tale castle with dragons | As a shabby, dingy little place | As a cosy hole in a river bank
exercise: Why does the girl laugh at that description? | She doesn't want to buy it; she wants something real | She thinks the house sounds simply dreadful | She has been there, and it is really very small and shabby | Toad sings the whole thing like an opera
exercise: Which part of Toad's real description does she like especially? | The linen-presses | The pig-styes | The golf-links | The coach-house full of motor-car bits
exercise: What does «sanguine» mean? | Cheerful and hopeful | Gloomy and sad | Very hungry | Hot-tempered and rude
exercise: Why does the girl never say she is fond of animals «as pets»? | She sees that Toad would be very offended | She doesn't really like animals at all | Her father has forbidden her to say it | She is secretly afraid of toads and frogs and all such creatures
exercise: What does vain Toad wrongly think about the girl? | That her kindness comes from great admiration for him | That she wants to keep him in prison for ever | That she is a spy sent by the police to trap him | That she wants to steal his motor-cars
exercise: Looking back: in chapter VI, what did Toad say about the Rat after he escaped from Toad Hall? | «very little intelligence and absolutely no education» | «the wisest and best animal on the whole river» | «my dearest friend, whom I shall never trick again» | «a fine fellow who should have a motor-car of his own»
exercise: Looking back: why did the magistrates give Toad twenty years? | For stealing a car, driving dangerously and cheeking the police | For robbing a bank and the village post-office | For burning down the Red Lion inn and its stables and coffee-room | For breaking out of Badger's house in the Wild Wood
exercise: Looking back: in chapter V, what supper did the Rat and the Mole find in the cupboards of Mole End? | Sardines, captain's biscuits and a German sausage | Bubble-and-squeak and hot buttered toast | Cold chicken, ham and pickled gherkins | Oatmeal porridge in wooden bowls and slices of fried ham
exercise: ★★★ «This is the end of everything ... at least it is the end of the career of Toad, which is the same thing». What does «which is the same thing» show? | He thinks his own troubles are the end of the whole world | He is being modest about himself | He means the castle is falling down | He means the book is ending
exercise: ★★★ Why is «very unique» a funny thing to say? | Unique means the only one; nothing can be «very» only one | Unique means ugly, so Toad insults his own house | Unique is French, and Toad cannot speak French | Unique means cheap and small, and Toad Hall is huge and very dear
test: «Toad's just chillin' in prison, eating loads from day one.» Put it right. | For weeks he refused his meals and wept | Yes: he ordered a feast every single day | He ate only bread and water from the start | He ate the gaoler's canary on the first day
test: Sophie, a bit mardy (sulky): «the gaoler's daughter was proper mean to Toad.» Put it right. | She was kind: she pitied him and brought him good food | She shouted at him through the keyhole every morning | She had him moved into an even darker cell | She stole all his money from his coat pocket while he slept
test: «That toast looks peng.» (peng = tasty) What does the toast do for Toad? | He sits up, eats, and talks happily about himself | He cries even harder, thinking of home and all his lost friends | He feels sick and pushes the plate away | He plans to rob the castle that very night
test: Which is TRUE about this part? | Toad describes Toad Hall as if he were selling it | The girl wants to buy Toad Hall for her aunt | Toad has never been inside Toad Hall at all | The old gaoler is very fond of Toad and his airs
test: Why does the girl say «Bless the animal ... I don't want to take it»? | Toad talks like a house-seller, though nobody is buying | She wanted to rent it but finds it far too dear | She was planning to steal it while he is in prison | She has already been there on a day out and didn't like it
test: What does the cabbage smell show about Toad? | His moods swing fast: one good smell, and he's hopeful again | He hates cabbage more than anything in the world | He is allergic to cabbage and sneezes | He never changes his mood at all, whatever happens
test: Ailsa from Edinburgh: «prison's dreich and Toad stays miserable the whole chapter.» (dreich = dreary) Put it right. | By that very night he is cheerful and pleased with himself again | Yes, he stays miserable right to the end of the chapter | He is miserable, but only on Sundays and on wet, dreary days | He grows so sad that he stops talking for ever
test: Grandpa from Ohio: «Don't have a cow, Toad's a gentleman, so he IS better than a jailer's girl.» (don't have a cow = calm down) What is right? | That's an old snobbish idea: being richer doesn't make anyone better | Yes: rich people really are better than everyone else, always | Yes: jailers and their families are bad people | No: the girl is really a princess in disguise
test: Why does the girl let Toad talk so much about himself? | She sees it does him as much good as the tea | She wants to write a book about his life | She is bored and has nothing else to do | She wants to report his secrets to the police
test: «Girl, you ate!» (ate = did brilliantly) Who «ate» in this part, and why? | The girl, who cured Toad's despair with food and kind talk | The gaoler, who sold Toad comforts at a good price | Toad, who escaped from the castle in the first week | The canary, who sang Toad to sleep every night from its nail on the wall
test: Looking back: who said in chapter VI that talking would never cure Toad, because «He'll say anything»? | The Rat | Badger | The Mole | The Clerk
test: Looking back: which earlier scene also shows Toad's moods swinging fast? | Sobbing in Badger's smoking-room, then saying «I'm not sorry» | Rowing calmly with the Rat for a whole summer day | Helping the Mole with his spring-cleaning | Singing carols with the field-mice in the snow
test: Looking back: who said at the end of chapter VI that Toad might be brought back «between two policemen»? | Badger | The Mole | The Rat | The gaoler
test: Looking back: Toad's yellow caravan in chapter II also had lockers full of food. Which was in them? | Biscuits, potted lobster and sardines | Bubble-and-squeak and buttered toast | A German sausage in silver paper | A yard of long French bread and cheese
test: Looking back: in chapter II the Rat said Toad was «both boastful and conceited». Which line here shows that best? | «an eligible self-contained gentleman's residence very unique» | «Father! I can't bear to see that poor beast so unhappy» | «Bless the animal, I don't want to take it» | «I'll make him eat from my hand, and sit up»
test: ★★★ Toad's sentence was twenty years, and he has been in prison «several weeks». How much of it is left? | Nearly all of it: almost twenty years | About half of it: ten years | Only a few more days | None: it is already over
test: ★★★ Careful: who says «You let me have the managing of him», and to whom? | The daughter, to her father | The father, to his daughter | Toad, to the old gaoler | The aunt, to the daughter
exam: Identify the condition in which the prisoner passed the initial weeks of his confinement. | In lamentation and despair, declining his meals | In cheerful industry, assisting the gaoler with his duties | In the composition of letters to his legal advisers | In the planning of an escape by means of a tunnel
exam: Ascertain the agency by which the prisoner's despondency was first alleviated. | The odour of hot cabbage, borne in a dish of bubble-and-squeak | The receipt of a letter from the Badger promising rescue | A visit from the Chairman of the Bench of Magistrates | The music of the canary hung upon the wall of the keep
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the prisoner's first account of his residence. | An account in the manner of an advertisement for the sale of property | A modest acknowledgement that the house was small and shabby | A refusal to discuss the said residence upon any terms | A description of a hole in a river bank above flood level
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the prisoner's opinion of the gaoler's daughter? | He wrongly supposed that her kindness proceeded from admiration for himself | He suspected her of acting as an informer to the police | He regarded her as a person of superior station to himself | He was wholly unaware of her existence until his release
